For $0\le n\le6Dv_b$, as a quadratic-form inequality on the bosonic $n$-particle Neumann space, $$\label{eq:rough-energy}
\begin{split}
 H_b\ge{}&\frac{4\pi an^2}{v_b}-Cv_bD^{1-0.001}
       +c\,\mathrm d\Gamma(\min\{p^2,1\})\\
 &+cD\sum_{A\in\mathcal C_d,\ A\subset b}|A|
    \mathfrak h\left(q_A-\frac{n}{Dv_b}\right),
 \qquad \mathfrak h(y)=\frac{y^2}{1+|y|}.
\end{split}$$ The same inequality holds with Hilbert-space-valued spectator variables.

For almost every exposed datum $\mathcal D$, the conditional law of the paths with labels in $I$ is $$\label{eq:conditional-path-law}
 \frac1{Z(\mathcal D)}\prod_{i\in I}J_i(\omega_i)
 \prod_{\substack{i<k\\\{i,k\}\cap I\ne\varnothing}}
       \mathbf 1_{\{u_{ik}\le a_{ik}(\omega)\}}
                    \prod_{i\in I}B_i(\,\mathrm d\omega_i),
 \qquad Z(\mathcal D)>0.$$ Frozen paths in this formula are held at their exposed values. Every pair indicator can fail only if the two paths approach within distance $r$ at some time.

The constants in the preceding definitions can be chosen so that $$\label{eq:good-prob}
\begin{aligned}
 \mathbb P(z\text{ is bad})&\le C_xD^{-1-1/8},\\
 \mathbb P(\text{every site in }A\text{ is bad})&\le p^{|A|}
                 &&(A\subset\mathcal L),\\
 \mathbb P(\text{full-path bounds fail in a fixed neighborhood})
                  &\le C_xD^{-6}.
\end{aligned}$$ In the second line $p>0$ may be any sufficiently small fixed number, after increasing the threshold for $D$. The constants and thresholds are uniform in the position of the neighborhood and in the volume.

Fix a trial label $i$ with eligible endpoints and a distinct opponent $k$ satisfying the motion cutoff. The $B_i$-probability that the trial satisfies its individual cutoffs and comes within $r$ of the opponent is at most $C\beta_{ik}$. The same holds for a trial pinned at a point $y$ within $8b_0$ of $m_i$, provided $d_K(y,\omega_k(0))>s_1$, with the additional cost $$\label{eq:midpoint-cost}
                   m(y,\omega_k)=
                       \frac{b_D}{s_1\vee d_K(y,\omega_k(0))}.$$ If instead $y$ is uniform in a unit cell at an absolute bounded distance from $m_i$, entirely within this pin range, the integrated cost is at most $C_x\beta_{ik}$, including failure of the midpoint separation condition. These bounds also bound failures of <a href="#eq:soft-check" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:soft-check">[eq:soft-check]</a>, uniformly in its exposed auxiliary variable.

The local energy estimates imply $$\label{eq:flag-moments}
 \begin{aligned}
 \mathbb E_{v\in\mathcal C_R}\mathbb E|A_v-1|^2
   &\le C\bigl(D^{-1-w/6}+D^{-1+x}R^{-3}\bigr),\\
 \mathbb E_{v\in\mathcal C_d}\mathbb E|A_v-1|^4
   &\le C_j\bigl(D^{-1-u/6}+D^{-2+2x}\bigr).
 \end{aligned}$$ Let $$M_v'=\frac1{T_v}\sum_{i\text{ flagged},\ i\notin I}h_{vi}$$ be the relative count discarded when only tags in $I$ are retained. Uniformly over cells of either size, $$\label{eq:tag-omission}
                    \mathbb EM_v'\le C_{x,j}D^{-1-1/8}.$$ In expectations involving a fixed number of cells, each additional relative-count factor can be bounded by $C_jD^x$, at an error smaller than any prescribed power of $D^{-1}$. The same assertion holds for fixed products of such factors.

Include the second tag indicator but omit its divisor $T_{w'}$. On admissible output paths the left likelihood is $$\label{eq:local-likelihood}
 L_S(z;\eta)=\int c_S(o;\eta)h_{w'i_m}(o)
                      k_S(o,\eta,z)B_S(\,\mathrm do).$$ It obeys $$\label{eq:local-centering}
 F_S=L_S-h_{w'i_m},\qquad
                 \int F_S\,\,\mathrm d\lambda_S(\cdot\mid\eta)=0,$$ and $$\label{eq:local-likelihood-bound}
                     0\le L_S\le C_xD^{C\sqrt x}.$$ These assertions remain valid after deleting specified outside checks consistently from the reference, the original-path integral, and all insertion tests and their normalizing probabilities.

Suppose the four changed sets are either pairwise disjoint, or have exactly one independent label identification. Write $\Delta_e$ for the finite difference from $\sigma_e=0$ to $\sigma_e=1$. Uniformly in all other switch settings, $$\label{eq:local-one-switch}
 \frac{|\Delta_eG|}{Z_U}
       \le C_xD^{C\sqrt x}\sum_{(l,k)\in\mathcal Q_e}\beta_{lk}.$$ If the changed sets are pairwise disjoint and $e\ne f$, then $$\label{eq:local-two-switches}
 \frac{|\Delta_e\Delta_fG|}{Z_U}
   \le C_xD^{C\sqrt x}
       \sum_{\substack{(l,k)\in\mathcal Q_e\\
                       (l',k')\in\mathcal Q_f}}
                                 \beta_{lk}\beta_{l'k'}.$$ The sums have at most $C_x$ terms. Moreover $\beta_{lk}\le C_xD^{-19/100}$ for sufficiently large $D$.

For sufficiently small fixed $x>0$, depending on $u$, the common measure constructed above satisfies $$\label{eq:local-lift-fourth}
 \mathbb E_{v\sim w'}\left[
       \int|\ell_v-1|^4\,\,\mathrm dP_{\mathrm{aug},v}
       +\int|\ell_{w'}-1|^4\,\,\mathrm dP_{\mathrm{aug},w'}\right]
                         \le C_jD^{-1-u/8}.$$ Consequently the local comparison <a href="#eq:local-fourth" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:local-fourth">[eq:local-fourth]</a> holds. That bound, with a fixed change of constant, also holds when only adjacent pairs inside the same $R$-cell are retained in the average.

On $\mathcal L_K=(\mathbb Z/K\mathbb Z)^3$, let $d_\infty$ be the periodic maximum distance. A random datum $\mathcal D$ determines a bad set $\mathcal B\subset\mathcal L_K$ satisfying $$\mathbb P(A\subset\mathcal B)\le p^{|A|}\qquad(A\subset\mathcal L_K).$$ Fix an integer contact radius $r_c\ge1$, an integer $q\ge0$, and $c_Q>0$. Let $Q$ be a projected lattice cube of side at most $c_QR$, and let $z$ be a site such that every $y\in Q$ has forward first-coordinate displacement from $z$ in $[.29K,.46K]$. There are constants $p_0,\kappa,c,C,C_0>0$ and $R_0$, depending only on the fixed parameters, with the following properties when $R\ge R_0$, $K\ge C_0R$, and $p\le p_0$.*

*Put $H=\lfloor R^{1/2}\rfloor$. There is an event $\mathcal E$, determined by $\mathcal D,z,Q$, on which one can sample simple good nearest-neighbor routes $\pi_y$ from $z$ to every $y\in Q$. Conditional on $\mathcal D$, different samples use independent fresh randomness. The event $\mathcal E$ includes the requirement that the $10R$-neighborhoods of $z$ and $Q$ contain no bad sites.*




*For $\pi=(v_0,\ldots,v_m)$ starting at $z$, put $$a(\pi)=\max\{i:d_\infty(v_i,z)\le H\},\qquad
 \pi^{\rm ch}=\{v_i:i>a(\pi)\}.$$ The initial portion $(v_0,\ldots,v_{a(\pi)})$ has at most $CH$ vertices. The analogous portion read backwards from the terminal site also has at most $CH$ vertices, and these two portions are disjoint. Let $\pi^{\rm ch,q}$ contain the vertices whose path index is within $q$ of an index in $\pi^{\rm ch}$. For independent samples $\pi_y,\pi_{y'}$, set $$J_c=\sum_{v\in\pi_y}\sum_{v'\in\pi_{y'}}
       \mathbf 1_{\{d_\infty(v,v')\le r_c\}},\qquad
 J_*=\sum_{v\in\pi_y^{\rm ch,q}}
       \sum_{v'\in\pi_{y'}^{\rm ch,q}}
       \mathbf 1_{\{d_\infty(v,v')\le r_c\}}.$$ Uniformly in the specified endpoints, $$\label{eq:route-exponential}
 \mathbb E_{\mathcal D}\bigl[\mathbf 1_{\mathcal E}
       \mathbb E_{\rm routes}(e^{\kappa J_c}\mid\mathcal D)\bigr]\le C.$$ For every deterministic probability mass function $\omega$ on $Q\times Q$ with $\omega(y,y')\le C_\omega R^{-6}$, $$\label{eq:route-rare}
 \sum_{y,y'\in Q}\omega(y,y')
   \mathbb E_{\mathcal D}\bigl[\mathbf 1_{\mathcal E}
       \mathbb P_{\rm routes}(J_*>0\mid\mathcal D)\bigr]
 \le CC_\omega R^{-c_g},\qquad
 c_g=\frac12\left(\frac{\log2}{\log3}-\frac12\right)>0.$$ Both estimates hold with a common terminal endpoint, reading the same route construction in reverse order. Finally, if $\mathcal M$ is the stated bad-free margin event, then $$\label{eq:route-rejection}
 \mathbb P(\mathcal M\setminus\mathcal E)
 \le K^3(Ap)^{\lfloor K/(10(r_c+2))\rfloor}+CR^3e^{-cR},$$ where $A$ is fixed and $Ap_0<1$.

For these conditional reference laws, $$\label{eq:long-normalizer}
 \frac{Z_S^-Z_T^-}{Z_U}
       \le C^{|S\cap T|}e^{CB_{ST}}.$$ If $S\cap T=\varnothing$, then for any measurable $0\le F\le1$ of the latent originals, $$\label{eq:reference-difference}
 0\le \frac1{Z_U}\int F(A_S^-A_T^--c_U)\,\,\mathrm dB_U
          \le CB_{ST}e^{CB_{ST}}.$$

For a finite nonnegative array $(y_i)$, with uniform independent indices $i,i'$, one has $$\label{eq:rms-fourth-inequality}
 \left(\sqrt{\mathbb E_i y_i^2}-\mathbb E_i y_i\right)^2
 \le\frac12\mathbb E_{i,i'}
          \frac{|y_i-y_{i'}|^4}{y_i^2+y_{i'}^2},$$ where the quotient is zero when its denominator vanishes. Moreover, $$\label{eq:square-root-quotient}
 |\sqrt y-\sqrt z|^4\le\frac{|y-z|^4}{y^2+z^2}
 \le8|\sqrt y-\sqrt z|^4\qquad(y,z\ge0).$$
